      {
        "id": 194609,
        "name": "robb8888",
        "username": "robb8888",
        "avatar_template": "/letter_avatar_proxy/v4/letter/r/dec6dc/{size}.png",
        "created_at": "2017-04-06T04:35:13.000Z",
        "cooked": "<aside class=\"quote group-playtesters\" data-username=\"McBehrer\">\n<div class=\"title\">\n<div class=\"quote-controls\"></div>\n<img loading=\"lazy\" alt=\"\" width=\"24\" height=\"24\" src=\"https://forums.greaterthangames.com/user_avatar/forums.greaterthangames.com/mcbehrer/48/1850_2.png\" class=\"avatar\"> McBehrer:</div>\n<blockquote>\n<p>I'll admit, I would prefer to also have the option to put the card back on top, but I still think it's far from useless.</p>\n</blockquote>\n</aside>\n<p>By \"useless,\" I mean \"puts you in just about the same situation you would have been in if you hadn't used it.\"</p>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n<p>SPOILER ALERT:</p>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n<p>Case in point: &nbsp;On the very first turn in this one-shot, I used RC Wraith's power, which revealed Police Backup. &nbsp;That's a (rare) good card for the heroes, so I played it. &nbsp;But, that also meant (a) that I still had a great unknown waiting on top of the environment deck, and (b) since one of the Police Backups had already been played, it meant there was a <em>greater</em> probability that the card actually waiting to be played was a bad one.</p>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n<p>Whereas, if I had just left well enough alone, Police Backup would have played anyway on the environment turn, and I'd've had one more turn free of nastiness from the environmental deck. &nbsp;Playing Police Backup early didn't provide any benefit, and actually hastened (by one card/turn) the coming of the bad cards in the deck.</p>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n<p>I'm really curious about the actual statistics/probability analysis behind this – whether, in the long run, it is actually better <em>never</em> to use RC Wraith's power. &nbsp;It may depend upon the number of \"really bad\" cards in the environment deck vs. the number of \"sorta bad/kinda good\" ones.</p>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>",
        "post_number": 23,
        "post_type": 1,
        "updated_at": "2021-02-07T05:21:47.720Z",
        "reply_count": 0,
        "reply_to_post_number": null,
