      {
        "id": 194611,
        "name": "Arcanist Lupus",
        "username": "Arcanist_Lupus",
        "avatar_template": "/user_avatar/forums.greaterthangames.com/arcanist_lupus/{size}/2169_2.png",
        "created_at": "2017-04-06T05:44:08.000Z",
        "cooked": "<p>Mint. &nbsp;It was pretty tight at first, but I managed to clean up the minions just before AZ would have kicked the bucket. &nbsp;Everyone was in the low teens except for AZ down at 2. &nbsp;Then a well placed Hoarfire bounced him back up to 16. &nbsp;Game ended with everyone around 9-11, just as I got to double Forced Deployments which Wraith's single Infrared Eyepiece couldn't handle.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>Regarding RC Wraith, it is perhaps useful to realize that because the environment deck is entirely randomized and the card that is looked at is removed from the deck, a power which looked at the bottom card of the deck rather than the top card would have a stastically identical effect. &nbsp;So you can see that (at least until around the 7th round when the power use affects whether or not you reshuffle) Wraith's power doesn't really effect the cards that you get. &nbsp;What it does do is a) gives you a pool of cards that you can put into play if you wish, and b) give you information about what is left in the deck. &nbsp;</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>So you can't use Wrath's sleuthing to avoid bad cards, but you can use it to get good cards, and to know how likely it is that certain cards will appear, making it easier to plan around them.</p>",
        "post_number": 25,
        "post_type": 1,
        "updated_at": "2021-02-07T05:21:47.858Z",
